# The Scoreboard's ignores. This is the only .gitignore in the repository.
#
# It used to say these rules lived in a root .gitignore one directory up and
# must not be repeated here. That was true while this was aici/scoreboard/
# inside the monorepo. The split left the sentence behind and the rules with
# the old repository, so for a while the only thing ignoring .env and .DS_Store
# here was a personal ~/.gitignore_global -- invisible from inside the project,
# and absent on everyone else's machine. An untracked .env holding an API key
# would have shown up as a file waiting to be added.
#
# The data product IS committed. outputs/scoreboard.db and the CSV exports
# beside it are what someone clones this repository for, so nothing below may
# exclude them.

# --- Recovered from the monorepo root on the split ------------------------- #
# Local environments and build artefacts. Not anchored to one directory: the
# README documents `python -m venv .venv` and it can be made anywhere.
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Credentials. .env is the reason this block is not optional.
.env
config.env
!.env.example

# macOS Finder droppings. Finder writes one into any directory it displays, so
# they appear without anyone touching the project. git-filter-repo also counts
# ignored files when deciding whether a clone is fresh, so a stray one can stop
# a future split until it is removed.
.DS_Store

# SQLite writes these beside the database while a transaction is open and
# removes them on commit. They are never part of the data, and a stray one left
# behind by a crashed run must not be committed.
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# The README tells you to copy the database before experimenting, because most
# commands open it read-write. Those copies stay local. The canonical database
# is the one exception.
*.db
!outputs/scoreboard.db

# Run transcripts from collect/. A routine run is noise -- large, append-only,
# and it changes every time, so committing them all would bury the diffs that
# matter. The transcript of a run that produced PUBLISHED data is a different
# thing: it is the provenance record for those rows, and it gets allowlisted
# here by name, the same way the canonical database is above.
**/logs/*.log
# Beside each transcript: the per-iteration token ledger, and (VERBOSE=1)
# the raw stream events. `**/logs/*.log` does not cover either -- they would
# arrive untracked-but-not-ignored and get staged by a plain `git add`.
**/logs/*.jsonl

# Answers from the review screen's agentic check, one JSON file each, so asking
# the same question twice costs one API call. The only thing under outputs/ that
# is NOT part of the data product, and the exception is deliberate: it sits
# beside the row it is about, and it is a cache, not a record. What a model said
# while a person was deciding is not evidence -- verify_verified holds what the
# person concluded, and that is what gets committed. Delete this directory at any
# time; the only consequence is that the next check pays full price.
outputs/agent_cache/

# Working files from collect/. Each run writes the lead or row it is about to
# ingest as JSON, then calls source-add / screen-add on it. Once the command
# returns, the database holds the content and the file is just the envelope it
# arrived in. The README is tracked so the directory exists in a fresh clone.
#
# `**/scratch/*` and not `scratch/*`: a leading `**/` matches at any depth, while
# a pattern containing a slash is anchored to the repo root. A collection call
# once wrote its lead to `scoreboard/scratch/lead.json` -- prefixing the repo's
# own name -- and the root-anchored rule did not cover it, so a working file
# appeared in `git status` asking to be committed. The prompts were fixed; this
# is the belt to that pair of braces.
**/scratch/*
!scratch/README.md
